{"id":65,"course_id":20,"title":"STS, AssumeRole, and Trust Policies","summary":"Follow identity handoffs across accounts and automation.","sequence":2,"body_simplified":"## Mental model\nTrust policies decide who can assume a role; permission policies decide what the role can do after assumption. CI, EKS controllers, and human break-glass flows depend on this separation.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice reading assume-role events, external IDs, conditions, and session names.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nReview a cross-account deploy role and identify who can assume it and what they can change.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","body_traditional":"## Mental model\nTrust policies decide who can assume a role; permission policies decide what the role can do after assumption. CI, EKS controllers, and human break-glass flows depend on this separation.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice reading assume-role events, external IDs, conditions, and session names.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nReview a cross-account deploy role and identify who can assume it and what they can change.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","pinyin":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-aws-iam-for-eks","course_category":"AWS IAM","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":296,"simplified":"AWS IAM for EKS and Platform Teams","traditional":"AWS IAM for EKS and Platform Teams","pinyin":"AWS IAM","definition":"Learn IAM policies, roles, trust relationships, STS, IRSA, Pod Identity, and least-privilege reviews for EKS workloads."},{"id":297,"simplified":"Evidence","traditional":"Evidence","pinyin":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":298,"simplified":"Blast radius","traditional":"Blast radius","pinyin":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":299,"simplified":"Rollback","traditional":"Rollback","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":300,"simplified":"Runbook","traditional":"Runbook","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":178,"lesson_id":65,"prompt":"What is the main goal of STS, AssumeRole, and Trust Policies?","answer":"Follow identity handoffs across accounts and automation.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":179,"lesson_id":65,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":180,"lesson_id":65,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
